         <div type="bio">
            <p>Prominent Danish scholar, known as a pioneer of the “Modern Breakthrough” in Scandinavian literature at the end of the nineteenth century. JJ admired Brandes’s <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Björnson och Ibsen</name> (1882) and <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">William Shakespeare: A Critical Study</name> (new ed., 1911), a copy of which JJ had in his library and which inspired Stephen’s theory of <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Hamlet</name> in <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Ulysses</name>. As Joyce writes in “Scylla and Charybdis”, “Mr Brandes accepts it, Stephen said, as the first play of the closing period”. <hi rend="bold">Dirk Van Hulle / William Brockman</hi>
                </p>
         </div>
